  "slug": "quantum-spin-81a6",
  "summary": "Four preprints revisit spin-liquid character, narrow-band magnetism and oxide gaps, reframing how ordered and disordered magnetic states are identified.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the reexamination of what defines magnetic ground states lacking conventional order, or deriving from nonstandard mechanisms. One preprint argues that frustrated magnets without low-temperature order are better termed quantum singlet liquids. Neural-network quantum state calculations on kagome Heisenberg clusters of up to 768 sites report a vanishing triplet gap and magnetic order, supporting a gapless U(1) spin liquid. Quantum geometry in narrow bands is shown, via Hartree-Fock, to drive altermagnetic and nematic phases through longer-range projected interactions. Parameter-free DFT on isolated NiO6 motifs in MgO yields intrinsic band gaps without long-range order, bearing on the Slater versus Mott question. The methods differ, but all challenge assumptions linking gaps, order and spin character.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
